from jsonb_to_recordset($blogdel$[{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-076","published_at":"2026-04-12 12:00:00+00","slug":"physical-activity-how-everyday-choices-affect-outcomes-076","title":"Physical activity: How everyday choices affect outcomes","description":"Physical activity explained through how everyday choices affect outcomes, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Physical activity: How everyday choices affect outcomes\n\nPhysical activity is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on how everyday choices affect outcomes while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nPhysical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## How everyday choices affect outcomes\n\nEveryday choices influence health through repeated exposure over time, but they operate alongside genetics, environment, access to care, work, income, and existing disease. For physical activity, small consistent behaviours are usually more meaningful than short periods of extreme effort. Age, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort. The aim is not perfection but a pattern that is sustainable and appropriate to the person's circumstances.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nAge, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPhysical activity is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Physical activity: How everyday choices affect outcomes","word_count":589,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Physical activity"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-077","published_at":"2026-04-13 12:00:00+00","slug":"physical-activity-when-professional-care-becomes-important-077","title":"Physical activity: When professional care becomes important","description":"Physical activity explained through when professional care becomes important, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Physical activity: When professional care becomes important\n\nPhysical activity is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on when professional care becomes important while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nPhysical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## When professional care becomes important\n\nSelf-care and general health information have limits. Professional care becomes important when symptoms are severe, persistent, progressive, unexplained, or are interfering with normal function. It is also important when a person is pregnant, very young, older and frail, immunocompromised, taking complex medicines, or living with conditions that change risk. New chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nAge, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPhysical activity is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Physical activity: When professional care becomes important","word_count":570,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Physical activity"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-078","published_at":"2026-04-14 12:00:00+00","slug":"musculoskeletal-health-how-it-works-078","title":"Musculoskeletal health: How it works","description":"Musculoskeletal health explained through how it works, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Musculoskeletal health: How it works\n\nMusculoskeletal health is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on how it works while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nMusculoskeletal health depends on bones, joints, muscles, tendons, ligaments, connective tissue, nerves, and the way loads are distributed during movement. Tissues adapt to appropriate loading, but injury can occur when forces exceed capacity or recovery is inadequate.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## How it works\n\nUnderstanding musculoskeletal health starts with the underlying system rather than with symptoms alone. Musculoskeletal health depends on bones, joints, muscles, tendons, ligaments, connective tissue, nerves, and the way loads are distributed during movement. Tissues adapt to appropriate loading, but injury can occur when forces exceed capacity or recovery is inadequate. A useful explanation therefore separates normal physiology, the factors that shift it, and the signs that suggest the system is no longer coping well.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nStrength, mobility, technique, prior injury, work demands, repetitive loading, sleep, nutrition, age, bone health, and recovery all influence risk. Pain does not always map directly to tissue damage, especially in persistent conditions.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nMajor trauma, deformity, loss of function, progressive weakness, new numbness with bladder or bowel symptoms, fever with a hot swollen joint, or inability to bear weight can require urgent assessment.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nMusculoskeletal health is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Musculoskeletal health: How it works","word_count":583,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Musculoskeletal health"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-079","published_at":"2026-04-15 12:00:00+00","slug":"musculoskeletal-health-why-prevention-matters-079","title":"Musculoskeletal health: Why prevention matters","description":"Musculoskeletal health explained through why prevention matters, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Musculoskeletal health: Why prevention matters\n\nMusculoskeletal health is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on why prevention matters while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nMusculoskeletal health depends on bones, joints, muscles, tendons, ligaments, connective tissue, nerves, and the way loads are distributed during movement. Tissues adapt to appropriate loading, but injury can occur when forces exceed capacity or recovery is inadequate.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## Why prevention matters\n\nPrevention matters because many health problems develop gradually, and the first opportunity to reduce harm often appears before severe symptoms. In musculoskeletal health, prevention can mean reducing exposure to known risks, maintaining protective routines, using recommended preventive services, and acting on early changes instead of waiting for crisis. Strength, mobility, technique, prior injury, work demands, repetitive loading, sleep, nutrition, age, bone health, and recovery all influence risk. Pain does not always map directly to tissue damage, especially in persistent conditions.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nStrength, mobility, technique, prior injury, work demands, repetitive loading, sleep, nutrition, age, bone health, and recovery all influence risk. Pain does not always map directly to tissue damage, especially in persistent conditions.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nMajor trauma, deformity, loss of function, progressive weakness, new numbness with bladder or bowel symptoms, fever with a hot swollen joint, or inability to bear weight can require urgent assessment.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nMusculoskeletal health is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Musculoskeletal health: Why prevention matters","word_count":589,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Musculoskeletal health"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-080","published_at":"2026-04-16 12:00:00+00","slug":"musculoskeletal-health-what-common-misconceptions-miss-080","title":"Musculoskeletal health: What common misconceptions miss","description":"Musculoskeletal health explained through what common misconceptions miss, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Musculoskeletal health: What common misconceptions miss\n\nMusculoskeletal health is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on what common misconceptions miss while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nMusculoskeletal health depends on bones, joints, muscles, tendons, ligaments, connective tissue, nerves, and the way loads are distributed during movement. Tissues adapt to appropriate loading, but injury can occur when forces exceed capacity or recovery is inadequate.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## What common misconceptions miss\n\nCommon misconceptions usually come from reducing a complex health topic to one rule, one symptom, or one product. Musculoskeletal health is not well explained by a single cause in every person. Musculoskeletal health depends on bones, joints, muscles, tendons, ligaments, connective tissue, nerves, and the way loads are distributed during movement. Tissues adapt to appropriate loading, but injury can occur when forces exceed capacity or recovery is inadequate. The more useful approach is to distinguish association from cause, screening from diagnosis, and general advice from individual clinical decisions.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nStrength, mobility, technique, prior injury, work demands, repetitive loading, sleep, nutrition, age, bone health, and recovery all influence risk. Pain does not always map directly to tissue damage, especially in persistent conditions.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nMajor trauma, deformity, loss of function, progressive weakness, new numbness with bladder or bowel symptoms, fever with a hot swollen joint, or inability to bear weight can require urgent assessment.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nMusculoskeletal health is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Musculoskeletal health: What common misconceptions miss","word_count":600,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Musculoskeletal health"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-081","published_at":"2026-04-17 12:00:00+00","slug":"musculoskeletal-health-how-risk-is-assessed-081","title":"Musculoskeletal health: How risk is assessed","description":"Musculoskeletal health explained through how risk is assessed, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Musculoskeletal health: How risk is assessed\n\nMusculoskeletal health is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on how risk is assessed while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nMusculoskeletal health depends on bones, joints, muscles, tendons, ligaments, connective tissue, nerves, and the way loads are distributed during movement. Tissues adapt to appropriate loading, but injury can occur when forces exceed capacity or recovery is inadequate.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## How risk is assessed\n\nRisk assessment combines multiple pieces of information rather than treating one result as destiny. For musculoskeletal health, clinicians and public-health professionals may consider age, symptoms, history, exposures, examination findings, trends over time, and tests that answer specific questions. Strength, mobility, technique, prior injury, work demands, repetitive loading, sleep, nutrition, age, bone health, and recovery all influence risk. Pain does not always map directly to tissue damage, especially in persistent conditions. Risk is therefore a probability estimate used to guide next steps, not a guarantee of what will happen.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nStrength, mobility, technique, prior injury, work demands, repetitive loading, sleep, nutrition, age, bone health, and recovery all influence risk. Pain does not always map directly to tissue damage, especially in persistent conditions.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nMajor trauma, deformity, loss of function, progressive weakness, new numbness with bladder or bowel symptoms, fever with a hot swollen joint, or inability to bear weight can require urgent assessment.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nMusculoskeletal health is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Musculoskeletal health: How risk is assessed","word_count":600,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Musculoskeletal health"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel